pire disintegrated into smaller states.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29776","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"776","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>Which instrument is associated with U. Srinivas?</span></p>","option_1":"Sitar","option_2":"Mandolin","option_3":"Tambura","option_4":"Nadaswaram","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Mandolin.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>U. Srinivas (Uppalapu Srinivas) was a renowned Indian musician celebrated for adapting the mandolin, ordinarily a Western instrument, to Carnatic classical music, popularising it as a solo concert instrument.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29777","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"776","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>_____ concluded that not only does the earth attract an apple and the moon, but all objects in the universe attract each other.</span></p>","option_1":"Albert Einstein","option_2":"Thomas Edison","option_3":"Isaac Newton","option_4":"Galileo Galilei","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Isaac Newton.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>Sir Isaac Newton formulated the Universal Law of Gravitation, stating that every object in the universe attracts every other object with a force proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them — famously inspired by the falling-apple observation.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29778","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"776","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>At which place was the Prarthana Samaj established in 1867?</span></p>","option_1":"Patna","option_2":"Bombay","option_3":"Calcutta","option_4":"Lucknow","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Bombay.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>The Prarthana Samaj, a socio-religious reform movement inspired by the Brahmo Samaj, was founded in Bombay in 1867 by Atmaram Pandurang; it was later led prominently by M.G. Ranade, focusing on social reforms like widow remarriage and the abolition of caste restrictions.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29779","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"776","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>As of July 2025, which of the following is NOT a classical language?</span></p>","option_1":"Kannada","option_2":"Odia","option_3":"Malayalam","option_4":"Hindi","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Hindi.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>As of 2025, India recognises eleven classical languages: Tamil, Sanskrit, Telugu, Kannada, Malayalam, Odia, Marathi, Bengali, Assamese, Pali, and Prakrit. Hindi does not hold classical language status under the Ministry of Culture's criteria (antiquity, ancient literary tradition, and originality).</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29780","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"776","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>Mahatma Gandhi organized a Satyagraha to support the peasants of Kheda. Kheda district is located in which of the following states?</span></p>","option_1":"West Bengal","option_2":"Maharashtra","option_3":"Gujarat","option_4":"Uttar Pradesh","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Gujarat.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>The Kheda Satyagraha (1918) was launched in Kheda district, Gujarat, where Gandhi, along with Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel, supported peasants seeking remission of land revenue after crop failure caused by drought and plague.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29781","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"776","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>Which of the following states has the lowest population density (per square km) as per census 2011?</span></p>","option_1":"Arunachal Pradesh","option_2":"Tamil Nadu","option_3":"Kerala","option_4":"Goa","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Arunachal Pradesh.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>As per Census 2011, Arunachal Pradesh recorded the lowest population density among Indian states, at around 17 persons per square kilometre, owing to its hilly terrain and low population in the north-eastern region.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29782","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"776","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>The universal law of gravitation states that the gravitational force between two objects is proportional to _____.</span></p>","option_1":"the difference of the masses","option_2":"the sum of the masses","option_3":"the product of the masses","option_4":"the square of the masses","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - the product of the masses.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>Newton's Universal Law of Gravitation is expressed as F = G(m1 m2)/r², meaning the gravitational force is directly proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centres.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29783","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"776","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>Subacute Sclerosing Panencephalitis (SSPE), highlighted during a protest in early 2025 at Mantralaya, is a rare, progressive and usually fatal disorder that affects which organ?</span></p>","option_1":"Liver","option_2":"Brain","option_3":"Heart","option_4":"Lungs","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Brain.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>SSPE is a rare, chronic, progressive neurological disorder caused by a persistent defective measles virus infection of the brain, occurring years after the initial measles infection, and it primarily affects the brain, leading to cognitive decline, seizures, and eventually death.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks